flowchart TD
  %% author: Brad W. Setser | outlet: CFR Expert Take | date: 2026-02-03
  %% title: Understanding the Low October 2025 Trade Deficit
  %% url: https://www.cfr.org/articles/understanding-the-low-october-2025-trade-deficit
  %% topics: US trade deficit, tariffs, stockpiling, fiscal policy | genre: Q&A explainer

  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["`**Question**
      Does the low October 2025 trade deficit mean the tariffs are working?`"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["`**Stated assumption**
      Tariffs reduce the trade deficit only if their proceeds reduce the budget deficit, acting as a tax hike`"]
    P2["`**Stated assumption**
      Remove pharmaceuticals and gold to read the underlying deficit`"]
    P3["`**Hidden assumption**
      *The trade balance is a macroeconomic savings-investment outcome; inventory swings reverse over time*`"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    E1["`**Data**
      October's drop came entirely from pharmaceuticals and gold; excluding them, October's deficit was slightly bigger than September's; August to October deficits below 2024, but early 2025 far above; imports of autos and household durable goods down; imports of semiconductors and computers up`"]
    E2["`**Studies and sources**
      U.S. trade data; Section 232 semiconductor investigation`"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["`**Tariff fears**
      Fear of tariffs that never came drove pharmaceutical stockpiling and gold flowing out and back`"]
    M2["`**Inventory drawdown**
      Stockpiles built ahead of tariffs temporarily depress imports, and stockpiles run out`"]
    M3["`**Untariffed artificial intelligence investment**
      The data center buildout keeps semiconductor and computer imports rising`"]
    M4["`**Fiscal channel**
      Promised refund checks and pressure for more defense spending block a fiscal adjustment`"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["`**Intermediate claim**
      Tariffs reduce imports in specific categories, but the overall deficit is driven by untariffed goods and stockpile timing`"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["`**Main claim**
      No: the low October deficit is not evidence the tariffs work, and a significant fall in the 2025 deficit is unlikely`"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Counterpoints"]
    X["`**Opposing view**
      Trump's trade team claiming success from smaller deficits than in the same months of 2024`"]
    QF["`**Caveat**
      Tariffed categories really are falling; watch whether imports rebound once stockpiles are exhausted`"]
    R1["`**Other explanation**
      Auto imports fell along with auto sales: weaker demand rather than more U.S. production`"]
  end

  P2 --> M1
  E1 --> M1
  E1 --> M2
  E1 --> M3
  E2 --> M3
  P1 --> M4
  M1 --> C1
  M2 --> C1
  M3 --> C1
  M4 --> K
  P3 -->|rule behind it| C1
  C1 --> K
  K -.->|pushes back on| X
  K -. caveat .- QF
  M2 -. other explanation .- R1

  Q ~~~ P1 & P2 & P3
  P1 ~~~ E1
  P3 ~~~ E2

